  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 5285559cfa33d7041aa12c3287c4d2a7, type: 3}
  m_Name: 08_CHECK_YOUR_UNDERSTANDING
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::NotebookSection
  number: 08
  title: CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING
  unlocked: 1
  entries:
  - title: Questions and answers
    tag: 0
    body: "<color=#E3C58F><b>Q.</b> What does the X gate do to |0\u27E9?</color>\n<b>A.</b>
      It turns it into |1\u27E9.\n\n<color=#E3C58F><b>Q.</b> A qubit in a 50/50 superposition
      is measured once. What do you see?</color>\n<b>A.</b> One result, 0 or 1, each
      with 50% probability. Never both.\n\n<color=#E3C58F><b>Q.</b> The console shows
      syndrome 01. Which qubit flipped, and what is the fix?</color>\n<b>A.</b> q2
      flipped. Apply X to q2.\n\n<color=#E3C58F><b>Q.</b> The data reads 010. What
      is the syndrome?</color>\n<b>A.</b> (0 \u2295 1, 1 \u2295 0) = 11, which points
      to q1.\n\n<color=#E3C58F><b>Q.</b> Why not measure the data qubits directly?</color>\n<b>A.</b>
      It would collapse any superposition. Parity checks reveal only relationships
      between qubits.\n\n<color=#E3C58F><b>Q.</b> Why can this code not fix a phase
      flip?</color>\n<b>A.</b> The parity checks compare bit values only, and a phase
      flip does not change them."
    bench: 5
    qiskit: 
